<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rosa Parks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rosa Louise McCauley Parks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rosa Parks was an American civil rights activist, best known for refusing on 1 December 1955 to give up her seat on a bus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, to a white passenger, in defiance of Jim Crow segregation laws. A seamstress and, from 1943, secretary of the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a> chapter, she was arrested and fined, and the bus boycott that followed lasted 381 days, until bus segregation was ruled unconstitutional in Browder v. Gayle. She moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> in 1957 and is sometimes called the &quot;mother of the civil rights movement&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-parks.jpg" alt="Rosa Parks" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Parks,_November_1956_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
